{#
| Variable                       | Type     | Description                                         |
|--------------------------------|----------|-----------------------------------------------------|
| page                           | object   |                                                     |
| page.title                     | string   |                                                     |
| page.slug                      | string   |                                                     |
| user.first_name                | string   |                                                     |
| user.last_name                 | string   |                                                     |
| user.birthday                  | ?date    | rendered by `salla-datetime-picker` — see T-5.05     |
| user.gender                    | ?string  | `male` · `female`                                    |
| user.email                     | string   |                                                     |
| user.mobile                    | string   |                                                     |
| user.avatar                    | ?string  |                                                     |
| custom_fields                  | Field[]  | merchant-defined, appended after the drawn fields    |
#}
{#
  T-5.04 — «حسابي», the profile hub.

  TWO CARDS, AND BOTH ARE THE SURFACE THE ACCOUNT AREA ALREADY HAS.
  `My Account Page.pdf` draws the form in one `.account-panel` and the
  deactivate-account block in a second, on the same warm `#F7F6F4` inside the
  same `#646361` border as the notifications and loyalty cards. **Nothing here
  declares a surface** — `.account-panel` and `.account-tile` come from
  `04-components/account-cards.scss`, and a third definition of that box would be
  the duplication doc 04 forbids.

  THE FIELDS BECOME THE DESIGN SYSTEM'S, WHICH IS THE BULK OF THE CHANGE.
  Upstream writes six hand-rolled `<label class="block text-sm font-bold …">` /
  `<input class="form-input">` pairs. T-2.06's `components.ui.input` is the
  theme's field — it carries the label association, the hint and error wiring,
  `aria-invalid`, the required marker and the autocomplete token. Routing the
  form through it is what makes «validation accessible» true for this page
  rather than asserted about it.

  THE GENDER CONTROL CHANGES SHAPE, AND THE ARTBOARD IS UNAMBIGUOUS. Upstream
  renders a `<select>` with a placeholder option; the design draws **two radio
  buttons**, «ذكر» and «أنثى», side by side. Two options are a radio group's
  exact case — a select for two mutually exclusive values hides one of them
  behind a tap. T-2.08's `components.ui.choice` is the control.

  WHAT THE BREADCRUMB SAYS IS A SLIP IN THE DRAWING, NOT AN INSTRUCTION. This
  artboard's breadcrumb reads «الرئيسية ‹ المفضلة» — the favourites page's trail
  on the account page's screen. `salla-breadcrumb` builds it from the platform's
  own page, so nothing here reproduces it. Recorded in DERIVED-DECISIONS.md.

  THE DEACTIVATE CARD IS `salla-user-settings`, AND TWO OF ITS THREE SECTIONS
  ARE HANDLED RATHER THAN IGNORED — see the note above it.
#}
{% extends "layouts.customer" %}

{% block inner_content %}
	<section class="account-panel profile-card" aria-labelledby="profile-form-title">
		<h2 class="sr-only" id="profile-form-title">{{ trans('theme.account.profile') }}</h2>

		{#
		  THE AVATAR IS UPSTREAM'S UPLOADER, SIZED TO THE ARTBOARD AND NOT
		  REPLACED. T-3.02 moved it here out of the layout and said explicitly
		  that styling it belonged to this task. The artboard draws a 99px
		  circle, centred, with a camera badge over its lower edge — the badge is
		  this task's addition and it is decorative: `salla-file-upload` already
		  carries the control and its accessible name.
		#}
		<div class="profile-avatar">
			<div class="profile-avatar__well">
				<salla-file-upload data-testid="store-profile-avatar-upload" profile-image value="{{ user.avatar }}">
					<span class="avatar-placeholder">
						{% include 'components.ui.icon' with { name: 'user', size: 'xl' } only %}
						<span class="text">{{ trans('common.uploader.choose_suitable_picture') }}</span>
					</span>
				</salla-file-upload>
				<span class="profile-avatar__badge" aria-hidden="true">
					{% include 'components.ui.icon' with { name: 'camera', size: 'sm' } only %}
				</span>
			</div>
		</div>

		{% hook 'customer:profile.form.start' %}
		<form class="form form--user-profile ajax profile-form"
		      onsubmit="return salla.form.onSubmit('profile.update', event)">
			<div class="profile-form__fields">
				{% hook 'customer:profile.form.fields.start' %}

				{% include 'components.ui.input' with {
					id:           'first_name',
					label:        trans('pages.profile.first_name'),
					value:        user.first_name,
					required:     true,
					autocomplete: 'given-name'
				} only %}

				{% include 'components.ui.input' with {
					id:           'last_name',
					label:        trans('pages.profile.last_name'),
					value:        user.last_name,
					required:     true,
					autocomplete: 'family-name'
				} only %}

				{#
				  T-5.05 owns this control. It is left as `salla-datetime-picker`
				  here — the component the backlog names — and restyled rather
				  than replaced, which is that task's own criterion.
				#}
				<div class="profile-form__field">
					<label class="field__label" for="birthday">{{ trans('pages.profile.birthday') }}</label>
					<salla-datetime-picker
						data-testid="store-profile-birthday"
						id="birthday"
						dateFormat="Y-m-d"
						value="{{ user.birthday }}"
						placeholder="{{ trans('pages.profile.birthday_placeholder') }}"
						name="birthday"
					></salla-datetime-picker>
				</div>

				{#
				  A radio group, as drawn — and a `<fieldset>` because that is what
				  gives the pair a shared, announced name. A `<label>` above two
				  radios names neither of them.
				#}
				<fieldset class="profile-form__field profile-gender">
					<legend class="field__label">{{ trans('pages.profile.gender') }}</legend>

					<div class="profile-gender__options">
						{% include 'components.ui.choice' with {
							id:      'gender-male',
							name:    'gender',
							type:    'radio',
							value:   'male',
							label:   trans('pages.profile.male'),
							checked: user.gender == 'male'
						} only %}

						{% include 'components.ui.choice' with {
							id:      'gender-female',
							name:    'gender',
							type:    'radio',
							value:   'female',
							label:   trans('pages.profile.female'),
							checked: user.gender == 'female'
						} only %}
					</div>
				</fieldset>

				{% include 'components.ui.input' with {
					id:           'email',
					type:         'email',
					label:        trans('common.elements.email'),
					value:        user.email,
					required:     true,
					autocomplete: 'email'
				} only %}

				{#
				  `salla-tel-input` is kept: it draws the flag and the +966 prefix
				  the artboard shows, and it owns country detection and the
				  formatting rules that go with it. Restyled from outside in
				  `04-components/profile.scss`, never reimplemented.
				#}
				<div class="profile-form__field">
					<label class="field__label" for="international-mobile">{{ trans('common.elements.mobile') }}</label>
					<salla-tel-input data-testid="store-profile-phone" phone="{{ user.mobile }}"></salla-tel-input>
				</div>

				{#
				  The merchant's own fields, after the drawn ones. Their markup is
				  upstream's because their shapes are the merchant's — a photo
				  uploader among them — and the artboard draws none of them, so
				  there is nothing here to match them against.
				#}
				{% for field in custom_fields %}
					<div class="profile-form__field profile-field-item">
						<label for="{{ field.id }}" class="field__label">{{ field.label }}</label>
						{% if field.type == 'photo' %}
							<salla-file-upload
								instant-upload
								value="{{ field.value }}"
								url="{{ store.url|trim('/'~user.language_code) }}/upload-image"
								name="custom_fields[{{ field.id }}]"
								payload-name="image"
								height="120px"
							>
								<div class="profile-filepond-placholder">
									<span class="profile-filepond-placholder-icon"><i class="sicon-camera"></i></span>
									<p class="profile-filepond-placholder-text">{{ trans('common.uploader.drag_and_drop') }}</p>
									<span class="filepond--label-action">{{ trans('common.uploader.browse') }}</span>
								</div>
							</salla-file-upload>
							<input type="hidden" name="custom_fields[{{ field.id }}]" value="{{ field.value }}">
						{% else %}
							<input type="{{ field.type }}"
							       id="{{ field.id }}"
							       value="{{ field.value }}"
							       name="custom_fields[{{ field.id }}]"
							       class="form-input" {{ field.required?'required':'' }}/>
						{% endif %}
					</div>
				{% endfor %}

				{% hook 'customer:profile.form.fields.end' %}
			</div>

			{% hook 'customer:profile.form.submit.start' %}

			{#
			  `salla-button type="submit"` is kept rather than swapped for T-2.05's
			  partial, and the reason is behaviour rather than tidiness: this
			  button is what `salla.form.onSubmit` drives — it calls `load()` and
			  `stop()` on it to show the request in flight, which is the save
			  state doc 04 asks for. A plain `<button>` would submit correctly and
			  then sit there saying nothing. It is restyled from outside instead.
			#}
			<salla-button data-testid="store-profile-submit" type="submit" loader-position="end" class="profile-form__submit">
				{{ trans('common.elements.save') }}
			</salla-button>

			{% hook 'customer:profile.form.submit.end' %}
		</form>
		{% hook 'customer:profile.form.end' %}
	</section>

	{#
	  `salla-verify` is the profile's own verification flow — the four-box code
	  T-2.07 built the theme's OTP for, on the `profile::verification` trigger.
	  It is a modal and draws nothing until the platform opens it, which is why no
	  artboard shows it and why it stays.
	#}
	<salla-verify data-testid="store-profile-verify"></salla-verify>

	{#
	  THE SECOND CARD IS ONE OF THIS COMPONENT'S THREE SECTIONS, AND THE OTHER TWO
	  ARE DECIDED RATHER THAN LEFT TO CHANCE.

	  `salla-user-settings` renders hide-my-name, promotional-messages and
	  delete-account. The artboard draws only «تعطيل الحساب».

	    · **promotional messages is hidden here, and nothing is lost:** the design
	      puts that switch on the notifications page, where T-5.08 built it
	      against the same `is_notifiable` field and the same `profile/settings`
	      write. Two controls for one setting on two pages is the thing to avoid.
	    · **hide-my-name is kept, against the artboard.** It is a privacy control
	      the platform ships and this theme offers nowhere else, and it only
	      appears at all for stores that enabled
	      `store.settings.rating.can_customer_hide_name`. Hiding a shipped
	      privacy control to match a drawing is a bigger decision than a template
	      should make quietly — the same call T-3.08 made for the VAT number.
	      Recorded as a deliberate deviation in DERIVED-DECISIONS.md.
	#}
	<section class="account-panel profile-settings" aria-labelledby="profile-settings-title">
		<h2 class="sr-only" id="profile-settings-title">{{ trans('theme.account.settings') }}</h2>

		<salla-user-settings data-testid="store-profile-settings" {{ user.is_notifiable?'is-notifiable':0 }}></salla-user-settings>
	</section>
{% endblock %}
